   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Saying what hurts",
     "texto": [
      "To say what hurts use **doler**. The thing that hurts is the **subject** and it usually comes after the verb:\n\n- One thing: **me duele** → **Me duele la cabeza.**\n- Several things: **me duelen** → **Me duelen los ojos.**\n\nFor a fever you do not use doler: you use **tener** → **Tengo fiebre.** And to ask: **¿Tienes fiebre?**\n\nIf you need medicine, ask for a **farmacia** and say it is **cerca**: **Hay una farmacia cerca.**"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Doler: the thing that hurts is the subject",
     "texto": [
      "The verb **doler** works like **gustar**:\n\n- It goes with a person: **me, te, le, nos, os, les**.\n- It is **singular** if one thing hurts: **me duele el estómago**.\n- It is **plural** if several things hurt: **me duelen los ojos**.\n\nThe verb agrees with the thing that hurts, not with the person. Before the body part use **el, la, los, las**, not «mi».\n\nA **fever** does not hurt: you have it → **tengo fiebre**."
     ],
